  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex but consistent relationship between 16-Epi-voacarpine and ME-180, mediated through multiple targets. The drug 16-Epi-voacarpine is directly linked to Histone H1.0, which in turn is a target of two distinct compounds: one is (2R,3S,4S,5R,6S)-2-(hydroxymethyl)-6-[4-[(E)-prop-1-enyl]phenoxy]oxane-3,4,5-triol, and the other is methyl (2R,6R)-6-[(5R,7S,10S,13R,14R,15S,17R)-7,15-dihydroxy-4,4,10,13,14-pentamethyl-3,11-dioxo-2,5,6,7,12,15,16,17-octahydro-1H-cyclopenta[a]phenanthren-17-yl]-2-methyl-4-oxoheptanoate. Both of these compounds ultimately target ME-180. Additionally, 16-Epi-voacarpine is also linked to Neuropilin-2, which is a target of Vitexin rhamnoside, which also targets ME-180. This suggests that 16-Epi-voacarpine may act through multiple pathways to influence ME-180, possibly through different molecular mechanisms. The evidence supports a role for 16-Epi-voacarpine in modulating ME-180 via its interaction with histone and neuropilin-related targets, highlighting a potential mechanism involving epigenetic regulation and receptor engagement.",
